<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Racibórz</h1><p class="st-meta">1281 CE – 1532 CE · capital Racibórz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Silesian duchy on the upper Oder, ruled first by Piasts and then by a Přemyslid branch from Opava until 1521.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1281 partition of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-opole-and-raciborz">Duchy of Opole and Racibórz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemyslaw-of-raciborz">Przemysław</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, the old capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-of-raciborz">Leszek</a> accepted Bohemian overlordship in 1327, and when Leszek died without heirs in 1336, the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii-of-opava">Nicholas II of Opava</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid</a> married to Przemysław's daughter.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslids</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> as part of a combined duchy of Opava and Racibórz, later separate. The duchy was a fertile land of grain and fish ponds, with a Polish-speaking population and German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>. It was a Bohemian fief, and its dukes took part in the Hussite wars and the struggles between Bohemian and Hungarian kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid</a> duke died in 1521, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-the-good">John II the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>. When he died in 1532, it fell to the Bohemian crown, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and pledged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1742 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-raciborz.jpg" alt="Duchy of Racibórz" width="320" height="363"><figcaption>Image: Zuber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silesia_1172-1177.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1281 CE – 1532 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Racibórz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Przemysław of Racibórz, Nicholas II of Opava, John II the Good</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish, Czech, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Ratibor, Duchy of Raciborz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1406&amp;focus=duchy-of-raciborz" aria-label="Duchy of Racibórz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5097 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Racibórz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5417 692L5408 675M5417 692L5330 692M5417 692L5410 696M5417 692L5408 675M5417 692L5428 702"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5408 675h0M5330 692h0M5410 696h0M5408 675h0M5428 702h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Racibórz. <a href="/atlas?year=1406&amp;focus=duchy-of-raciborz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
